WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.671
A language model can finish a sentence. How do you teach it to
answer a question,

00:00:04.671 --> 00:00:08.901
judge when to refuse, and remember an instruction several turns
later?

00:00:08.901 --> 00:00:12.980
Llama 2 tackles these problems by changing what the model
practices,

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.422
and by changing how its answers are judged.

00:00:16.072 --> 00:00:20.047
Start on the left. Meta pretrained Llama 2 on two trillion
tokens.

00:00:20.047 --> 00:00:24.381
A token is a piece of text. The model learned by predicting the
next piece.

00:00:24.381 --> 00:00:27.787
Its context window was four thousand ninety-six tokens.

00:00:27.787 --> 00:00:32.272
The thirty-four and seventy billion parameter models used
grouped-query attention.

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:37.674
Several query heads share keys and values, so generation can use
a smaller cache.

00:00:38.324 --> 00:00:42.125
Move one step to the right. Human-written examples show the
model

00:00:42.125 --> 00:00:44.288
how a good assistant might respond.

00:00:44.288 --> 00:00:48.228
The team collected twenty-seven thousand five hundred forty
annotations

00:00:48.228 --> 00:00:49.880
for supervised fine-tuning.

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:53.820
That gives the model a starting point. It can now draft replies

00:00:53.820 --> 00:00:57.992
for people to compare, and those comparisons become the next
training signal.

00:00:58.642 --> 00:01:02.164
One scorer learns which answers people find more helpful.

00:01:02.164 --> 00:01:05.151
Another learns which answers people find safer.

00:01:05.151 --> 00:01:09.660
The paper gives a routing rule for reward: use safety on a
safety prompt,

00:01:09.660 --> 00:01:13.611
or when the answer's safety score falls below zero point one
five.

00:01:13.611 --> 00:01:18.329
Otherwise use helpfulness. On borderline prompts, stronger
safety tuning

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:21.700
could also make the assistant refuse harmless requests.

00:01:22.349 --> 00:01:27.555
In rejection sampling, generate several replies and train on the
best-scored ones.

00:01:27.555 --> 00:01:32.655
In PPO, use the reward to update the probabilities of generated
replies,

00:01:32.655 --> 00:01:35.666
with a penalty for drifting too far from a reference model.

00:01:35.666 --> 00:01:39.095
Better models produce new replies for people to compare.

00:01:39.095 --> 00:01:42.826
The team iterated from RLHF version one to version five.

00:01:43.476 --> 00:01:48.693
Now follow the same assistant into a conversation. Say the
instruction is, answer in French.

00:01:48.693 --> 00:01:53.166
Ghost Attention repeats the instruction while generating
practice conversations.

00:01:53.166 --> 00:01:56.909
For fine-tuning, it keeps the instruction only on the first turn

00:01:56.909 --> 00:02:01.092
and trains on the final reply. Earlier turns provide context.

00:02:01.092 --> 00:02:06.042
The paper reports consistency for twenty or more turns, up to
the context limit.

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:10.991
Meta released base and chat weights in seven, thirteen, and
seventy billion

00:02:10.991 --> 00:02:13.689
parameter sizes under the Llama 2 license.

00:02:13.689 --> 00:02:17.466
The lesson is simple. Show the model what good behavior looks
like,

00:02:17.466 --> 00:02:21.522
score the behaviors you care about, and practice them in the
setting where they matter.
